event: content_block_delta
data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":61,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"## What safety guarantees have actually been proven — e.g. quantilisers (Taylor), impact measures, myopic/soft agents, safe RL shielding, verified policy synthesis — and what are their exact assumptions?\n\n### Takeaway\nSeveral genuine safety theorems exist, but each guarantees a bounded property of a formal proxy — expected cost relative to a base distribution, non-interference with interruptions, or satisfaction of a temporal-logic specification in a fixed model — not alignment with human values and not preservation under open-ended self-modification.\n\n### Cited Findings\n- **Quantiliser cost bound.** For a finite action set \\(A\\), utility \\(U:O\\to[0,1]\\), base distribution \\(\\gamma\\), and cost function \\(c\\) with \\(\\mathbb{E}_{A\\sim\\gamma}[c(A)]\\le 1\\), a \\(q\\)-quantiliser satisfies \\(\\mathbb{E}_{A\\sim Q_q}[c(A)]\\le \\frac{1}{q}\\mathbb{E}_{A\\sim\\gamma}[c(A)]\\) (Lemma 1); choosing \\(q=1/t\\), the \\(q\\)-quantiliser maximises expected \\(U\\)-utility subject to the conservative cost constraint \\(\\forall c:\\mathbb{E}_\\gamma[c]\\le1\\Rightarrow\\mathbb{E}_p[c]\\le t\\) (Theorem 1). — [Quantilizers PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/QuantilizersSaferAlternative.pdf)\n- **Quantilisers are a one-shot construction.** MIRI notes that ordinary quantilisers “behave poorly in repeated games,” producing very high or very low expected utility, and that safety depends strongly on how the action distribution \\(\\gamma\\) is specified. — [MIRI, “New paper: Quantilizers”](https://intelligence.org/2015/11/29/new-paper-quantilizers/)\n- **Safe interruptibility.** Orseau and Armstrong give a formal definition of safe interruptibility and prove that some agents are already safely interruptible (Q-learning), while others can easily be made so (Sarsa) by exploiting the off-policy learning property; they also show that even ideal, uncomputable RL agents for deterministic general computable environments can be made safely interruptible. — [UAI 2016 abstract](https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.5555/3020948.3021006)\n- **Shielding.** A shield is synthesised from the safety fragment of linear temporal logic plus an abstraction of the agent’s environment dynamics; it monitors the learner’s actions and corrects them iff the chosen action would violate the specification. The paper claims correctness of execution against the specification during learning and execution “regardless of how fast the learning process converges,” and discusses which requirements the shield must meet to preserve the learner’s convergence guarantees. — [AAAI 2018, “Safe Reinforcement Learning via Shielding”](https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/11797/11656)\n- **Verified policy synthesis (VSRL).** A safety guard replaces an unsafe proposed action with a uniformly sampled safe one, producing a refined MDP in which all actions are safe and whose optimal policies exactly correspond to the best safe policies in the original problem; a differential-dynamic-logic (dL) proof then provides a formal certificate of safety from the starting safe state. — [Frontiers 2026 systematic review](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/artificial-intelligence/articles/10.3389/frai.2026.1749956/full)\n- **Impact measures.** No theorem was found in the returned material proving that an impact measure (relative reachability, attainable utility preservation, impact regularisation) preserves alignment or goals; the material describes impact regularisation as a penalty/approximation of the true environment reward rather than a proven safety guarantee. — (status of evidence; see Gaps)\n\n### Inferences\n- Every proven result is relative to a fixed formal object: the cost function \\(c\\) for quantilisers, the temporal-logic safety property for shielding, the interruption definition for safe interruptibility, the refined MDP for VSRL. None of these objects is “human values” in general.\n- The guarantees are one-shot, per-step, or per-episode. None is an asymptotic or unbounded-time guarantee, and none quantifies over the agent changing its own objective, model, or learning rule.\n- Safe interruptibility is the closest formal result to a “myopic/soft agent” guarantee, but it guarantees only that the agent will not learn to avoid or seek interruption; it does not guarantee value alignment.\n\n### Gaps\n- No source returned in this search proves an alignment or goal-preservation guarantee for impact measures; impact regularisation appears as an empirical penalty, not a theorem.\n- No source returned proves a general “soft agent” or “myopic agent” alignment theorem; myopia is typically an assumption imposed on the agent, not a derived safety property.\n- No source returned extends quantiliser, shielding, or safe-interruptibility theorems to agents that modify their own objective, environment model, or learning algorithm.\n\n## Which formalisms provably DO tile or self-trust: e.g. logically uncertain agents, probabilistic reflection, logical induction (Garrabrant et al.), Benya Fallenstein & Jessica Taylor's results, Christiano et al. 'Definability of Truth in Probabilistic Logic', Demski's work — and what do they guarantee about alignment specifically?\n\n### Takeaway\nLogical induction, probabilistic reflection, and proof-based tiling are real self-trust/reflection theorems, but they guarantee coherence of beliefs, reflective consistency of a probability assignment, or preservation of a fixed formal utility function — not alignment with human values.\n\n### Cited Findings\n- **Logical induction.** The algorithm assigns probabilities to sentences of logic and has “a number of good inductive, deductive, and reflective properties”; in the limit it obeys all logical constraints and learns all logical facts; it “trusts its own probabilities, in the sense that, roughly speaking, it knows what it knows, and trusts its future beliefs to be more accurate than its current beliefs.” These properties follow from a single logical induction criterion: no polynomial-time-implementable trading strategy can exploit its prices. — [Logical Induction (abridged PDF)](https://intelligence.org/files/obsolete/LogicalInductionShort-2016-09-17.pdf)\n- **Logical induction is asymptotic and doxastic.** The guarantees are stated “in the limit,” and the object is a belief assignment, not an action policy or a value-preservation theorem. — [Logical Induction (abridged PDF)](https://intelligence.org/files/obsolete/LogicalInductionShort-2016-09-17.pdf)\n- **Definability of truth in probabilistic logic.** Although a language cannot contain its own truth predicate, it can contain its own subjective probability function \\(\\mathbb{P}\\). The paper’s stated goal is to show that “starting from any consistent theory \\(T\\) we can obtain a reflectively consistent \\(\\mathbb{P}\\) which assigns probability 1 to each sentence of \\(T\\),” using reflection conditions (3)–(4) and a Kakutani fixed-point construction. — [Definability of Truth in Probabilistic Logic](http://intelligence.org/files/DefinabilityTruthDraft.pdf)\n- **Tiling.** The report states that Fallenstein and Soares (2014) demonstrate that “certain types of proof-based agents” can “tile” — license the construction of successor agents similar to themselves while avoiding Gödelian diagonalization — “in environments about which the agent can prove some basic nice properties.” Soares (2014) extends the proof to causal graphs with a specific structure. — [MIRI, “Tiling agents in causal graphs”](https://intelligence.org/2014/12/16/new-report-tiling-agents-causal-graphs/); [Tiling Agents in Causal Graphs PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/TilingAgentsCausalGraphs.pdf)\n- **Exact tiling conditions.** The parent is a meliorising suggester-verifier with fallback policy \\(\\pi_{fb}\\) and utility \\(U\\). To license a child policy \\(\\pi'\\), the parent’s theory \\(T\\) must prove: (2) \\(\\pi'\\) is a meliorising suggester-verifier; (3) \\(\\pi'\\)’s fallback is at least as good as the parent’s; (4) a reflection schema \\(T\\vdash\\forall n\\in\\mathbb{N}.\\ \\Box_{T'}\\ulcorner\\varphi(n)\\urcorner\\to\\varphi(n)\\) — i.e. \\(T\\) proves the child’s verifier theory \\(T'\\) sound; and (5) the causal graph makes the child node implement \\(\\pi'\\) with probability 1. Observation and action sets are finite. — [Tiling Agents in Causal Graphs PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/TilingAgentsCausalGraphs.pdf)\n- **Vingean reflection.** The report describes proof-based reasoning about smarter successors as “the best currently available formal model of abstract reasoning,” while cautioning that “it is unlikely that real-world agents would base their behavior entirely on formal proofs.” — [MIRI, “Vingean Reflection”](https://intelligence.org/2015/01/15/new-report-vingean-reflection-reliable-reasoning-self-improving-agents/)\n\n### Inferences\n- “Self-trust” in logical induction and probabilistic reflection means trust in one’s own probability assignments, not trust that a successor will pursue human values.\n- The tiling theorem is the closest formal analogue of “alignment preserved under self-modification,” but it preserves the same formal utility \\(U\\) supplied to the model and assumes the parent can prove the child’s proof system sound. It says nothing about whether \\(U\\) encodes human values.\n- Logical induction and probabilistic reflection provide no action-selection guarantee at all, so they cannot by themselves guarantee alignment.\n\n### Gaps\n- No source returned gives a tiling or reflection theorem for an agent that modifies its own utility function or its own proof system in an open-ended way.\n- No source returned establishes an alignment guarantee from Demski’s work; the returned material framed embedded agency as a set of open problems rather than a proven alignment result.\n- Reflective oracles (Fallenstein, Taylor, Christiano) were not examined in full text in this task, so no detailed claim about their guarantees is made here.\n\n## Have debate, iterated amplification or recursive reward modelling been given any formal correctness guarantees, and under what assumptions?\n\n### Takeaway\nDebate has formal complexity-theoretic correctness theorems for a verifier with access to human judgements, but they concern certifying a claimed solution, not preserving alignment under self-modification. No formal correctness theorem for iterated amplification or recursive reward modelling was found; those approaches rest on empirical assumptions.\n\n### Cited Findings\n- **Doubly-efficient debate.** The paper defines a complexity-theoretic model in which two polynomial-time provers compete to convince a verifier who has access to black-box human judgements. It proves theorems in three settings: deterministic human judgements with explicit natural-language instructions, stochastic human judgements, and a setting where the models propose a solution and must justify its correctness. The honest strategy can succeed using a polynomial number of simulation steps, even when the dishonest strategy may use exponentially many; the main stochastic theorem is formalised in Lean 4. — [Scalable AI Safety via Doubly-Efficient Debate](https://arxiv.org/html/2311.14125)\n- **What the debate theorem covers.** The formal guarantees are that (1) the prover arguing for the correct solution can convince the verifier without much more computation than solving the problem, and (2) the verifier’s number of human-judgement queries is a fixed constant independent of problem size. — [Scalable AI Safety via Doubly-Efficient Debate](https://arxiv.org/html/2311.14125)\n- **Iterated amplification.** No formal correctness theorem was found. The returned material describes the method as an empirical training procedure whose paper is “Supervising Strong Learners by Amplifying Weak Experts,” with an implementation released to produce those empirical results. — [GitHub: rmoehn/amplification](https://github.com/rmoehn/amplification)\n- **Recursive reward modelling.** No formal correctness theorem was found. The returned material describes recursive reward modelling as a training technique that decomposes evaluation into easier sub-tasks, not as a proved alignment-preservation result. — (status of evidence; see Gaps)\n\n### Inferences\n- Debate’s formal content is epistemic: it helps a weak judge detect errors in a claimed solution. It does not model the debaters rewriting their own objectives, nor the judge’s values changing, nor an unbounded sequence of self-modifications.\n- Even if the debate protocol is correct, the guarantee is per-claim and conditional on an honest prover and a human-judgement oracle. It is not an indefinite alignment guarantee.\n- Iterated amplification and recursive reward modelling may be plausible oversight strategies, but in the sources found they are engineering proposals with empirical support, not theorems.\n\n### Gaps\n- No source returned states an asymptotic or unbounded-horizon alignment guarantee for debate, iterated amplification, or recursive reward modelling.\n- No source returned formalises recursive reward modelling’s “evaluation is easier than generation” assumption as a theorem; the returned material treats it as an empirical premise.\n- No source returned gives a formal model in which debate or amplification is applied to an agent that modifies its own policy or objective.\n\n## What do formal verification and runtime-monitoring approaches guarantee in bounded settings (model checking, abstract interpretation, shielding, proof-carrying code), and why do these guarantees not obviously extend to open-ended self-modification?\n\n### Takeaway\nFormal verification and runtime monitoring give strong guarantees relative to a fixed specification, model, and monitor, over bounded state spaces or finite traces. They do not survive an agent that can change the specification, model, monitor, or deployment environment, and even bounded neural-network verification is not practically sound in deployment.\n\n### Cited Findings\n- **Runtime verification.** It is “a lightweight formal method applied at runtime to provide rigorous guarantees of property adherence for a system,” at the cost of “limited execution coverage”; a monitor is generated from a formal specification and instrumented to check the system trace. — [Frontiers 2026 systematic review](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/artificial-intelligence/articles/10.3389/frai.2026.1749956/full)\n- **Verified policy synthesis.** The VSRL approach produces a refined MDP in which all actions are safe and optimal policies correspond exactly to the best safe policies; a dL proof gives a formal certificate of safety from the starting safe state. — [Frontiers 2026 systematic review](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/artificial-intelligence/articles/10.3389/frai.2026.1749956/full)\n- **Shielding.** The guarantee holds “regardless of how fast the learning process converges,” but the shield is computed from an abstraction of the environment and must satisfy requirements to preserve the learner’s convergence guarantees. — [AAAI 2018, “Safe Reinforcement Learning via Shielding”](https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/11797/11656)\n- **Neural-network verification is not practically sound.** The 2025 ICML paper claims that “all the state-of-the-art verifiers we are aware of fail to reach” the goal of guaranteeing the safety of deployed neural networks. Its key claim is that theoretical soundness — bounding the full-precision output while computing with floating point — does not imply practical soundness — bounding the floating-point output in a potentially stochastic environment; the paper proves this for interval analysis and its variants and shows that all tested verifiers are vulnerable to deployment-specific attacks. — [“No soundness in the real world,” ICML 2025](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/3780338.3782646)\n- **Abstract interpretation.** Interval analysis is one of the approaches the 2025 paper identifies as achieving theoretical soundness but failing practical soundness in deployment. — [“No soundness in the real world,” ICML 2025](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/3780338.3782646)\n\n### Inferences\n- These are “envelope” guarantees: they hold for behaviour inside a fixed envelope — a specification, an abstraction, a monitor, a proof checker, or a finite-state model. Self-modification that stays inside the envelope can be safe; self-modification that changes the envelope is outside the theorem.\n- The 2025 neural-network verification result shows that even the bounded guarantee is difficult to transfer from a mathematical model to a deployed floating-point system. The gap between “verified in the model” and “safe in the world” is already present without self-modification.\n- A self-modifying agent that can alter its own code but not the shield or monitor may remain safe with respect to the monitored interface; a self-modifying agent that can alter the specification or monitor voids the guarantee. The formal results found do not distinguish these cases.\n\n### Gaps\n- No source returned extends model checking, abstract interpretation, shielding, or runtime monitoring to an agent that can modify its own verifier, specification, or environment model.\n- Proof-carrying code was not examined in full text in this task; the general “fixed host safety policy” structure is noted here only as an envelope-type guarantee, not as a cited detailed result.\n- No source returned proves that a runtime monitor remains correct when the agent can influence the monitor’s observations or the specification it enforces.\n\n## Are there proofs that alignment is preserved under restricted self-modification (e.g. self-modification within a fixed verified envelope, or monotonically improving agents)?\n\n### Takeaway\nThe strongest formal preservation results are the tiling theorems for proof-based agents with a fixed utility function and the meliorising self-improvement condition: a self-modification is licensed only if the parent can prove it yields at least as much expected utility as the fallback. These preserve a formal objective \\(U\\), not human values, and depend on the proof system remaining sound.\n\n### Cited Findings\n- **Tiling as restricted self-modification.** A parent meliorising suggester-verifier can license the construction of a child policy \\(\\pi'\\) if it can prove: (2) \\(\\pi'\\) is a meliorising suggester-verifier; (3) \\(\\pi'\\)’s fallback is at least as good as the parent’s fallback; (4) \\(T\\) proves the child’s theory \\(T'\\) sound via the reflection schema \\(T\\vdash\\forall n.\\ \\Box_{T'}\\ulcorner\\varphi(n)\\urcorner\\to\\varphi(n)\\); and (5) the causal graph makes the child implement \\(\\pi'\\) with probability 1. — [Tiling Agents in Causal Graphs PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/TilingAgentsCausalGraphs.pdf)\n- **Monotonic improvement condition.** In the tiling model, to execute an action \\(a\\) the suggester must find a proof that \\(\\mathbb{E}[U\\mid O=o,\\ do(A:=a)]\\ge \\mathbb{E}[U\\mid O=o,\\ do(A:=\\pi_{fb}(o))]\\). The child’s fallback must also be at least as good as the parent’s, so each licensed step is non-decreasing in expected \\(U\\) relative to the fallback. — [Tiling Agents in Causal Graphs PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/TilingAgentsCausalGraphs.pdf)\n- **The preserved object is the formal utility function.** The tiling conditions explicitly require the child to use “the same utility function as the parent.” The theorem does not state that this \\(U\\) encodes human values, nor that \\(U\\) itself may be modified. — [Tiling Agents in Causal Graphs PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/TilingAgentsCausalGraphs.pdf)\n- **The result is an inductive step.** The proof licenses one child construction at a time; it does not prove that an infinite chain of successors preserves \\(U\\). The assumptions include finite observation and action sets and a fixed theory \\(T\\). — [Tiling Agents in Causal Graphs PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/TilingAgentsCausalGraphs.pdf)\n- **Safe interruptibility is preserved under interruptions, not self-modification.** The theorem guarantees that certain RL agents will not learn to avoid or seek interruption; it does not model the agent rewriting its own learning rule or objective. — [UAI 2016 abstract](https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.5555/3020948.3021006)\n\n### Inferences\n- The common structure is “fixed objective + sound proof system + verified implementation.” This is exactly a restricted self-modification envelope: any accepted modification is provably non-decreasing in \\(U\\) relative to the fallback.\n- Because the parent must prove the child’s theory sound, the result does not straightforwardly apply to a child that rewrites its own proof system in a way the parent cannot verify. The reflection schema is an assumption, not something the child’s self-modification establishes.\n- “Alignment preservation” here means preservation of \\(U\\). It is silent on whether \\(U\\) is the right objective, whether \\(U\\) is stable under reflection, or whether a real system’s successor would keep \\(U\\).\n\n### Gaps\n- No source returned proves preservation of human values, as opposed to a formal utility \\(U\\), under self-modification.\n- No source returned allows the agent to modify \\(U\\) itself while preserving alignment.\n- No source returned proves an infinite sequence of self-modifications preserves \\(U\\); the tiling theorem is a one-step licensing result, and its assumptions must hold at each step.\n- Gödel-machine-style self-improvement was not examined in full text in this task; no detailed claim about its formal guarantees is made here.\n\n## What is the gap between “a theorem in a toy formal model” and “a guarantee for a real self-improving system” — enumerate the assumptions that would have to be discharged.\n\n### Takeaway\nThe gap is a conjunction of assumptions: a fixed formal objective that adequately captures human values, a correct environment model or abstraction, a sound verifier that survives self-modification, verified implementation of the intended policy, bounded state/action/horizon, and a monitor or specification the agent cannot change. No source found discharges all of these simultaneously, and several results are only asymptotic or one-step.\n\n### Cited Findings\n- **Quantilisers require a base distribution and a cost function.** The bound is relative to \\(c\\), assumes \\(\\mathbb{E}_\\gamma[c]\\le1\\), uses finite action sets, and is a one-shot choice; MIRI notes poor behaviour in repeated games. — [Quantilizers PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/QuantilizersSaferAlternative.pdf); [MIRI, “New paper: Quantilizers”](https://intelligence.org/2015/11/29/new-paper-quantilizers/)\n- **Tiling requires provable soundness and verified implementation.** The parent must prove the child’s theory \\(T'\\) sound and that the causal graph makes the child implement \\(\\pi'\\) with probability 1. — [Tiling Agents in Causal Graphs PDF](https://intelligence.org/files/TilingAgentsCausalGraphs.pdf)\n- **Shielding requires a correct abstraction.** The shield is computed from an abstraction of the environment and must satisfy requirements to preserve convergence. — [AAAI 2018, “Safe Reinforcement Learning via Shielding”](https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/11797/11656)\n- **Neural-network verification: theoretical soundness is not practical soundness.** “Theoretical soundness (bounding the full-precision output while computing with floating point) does not imply practical soundness (bounding the floating point output in a potentially stochastic environment).” — [“No soundness in the real world,” ICML 2025](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/3780338.3782646)\n- **Debate requires an honest prover and a human-judgement oracle.** The correctness theorems are conditional on the honest strategy and on the verifier’s access to black-box human judgements. — [Scalable AI Safety via Doubly-Efficient Debate](https://arxiv.org/html/2311.14125)\n- **Logical induction’s guarantees are asymptotic and doxastic.** The algorithm’s good properties hold “in the limit,” and it is a belief-forming procedure, not an action-selection or value-preservation theorem. — [Logical Induction (abridged PDF)](https://intelligence.org/files/obsolete/LogicalInductionShort-2016-09-17.pdf)\n- **Probabilistic reflection is relative to a consistent theory.** The reflection result starts from “any consistent theory \\(T\\)” and produces a reflectively consistent probability assignment; it does not produce an aligned agent. — [Definability of Truth in Probabilistic Logic](http://intelligence.org/files/DefinabilityTruthDraft.pdf)\n- **Real-world agents are unlikely to be pure proof-based agents.** The Vingean reflection report states that “it is unlikely that real-world agents would base their behavior entirely on formal proofs.” — [MIRI, “Vingean Reflection”](https://intelligence.org/2015/01/15/new-report-vingean-reflection-reliable-reasoning-self-improving-agents/)\n\n### Inferences\n- Each theorem has the form “if assumptions \\(A\\) hold, property \\(P\\) holds in model \\(M\\).” For a real self-improving system, each \\(A\\) is an empirical or engineering claim — that the objective is correctly formalised, that the model is faithful, that the verifier is sound, that the implementation matches the verified policy, that the horizon is bounded, that the monitor is incorruptible. The conjunction is not established by any source found.\n- “Indefinitely” is not delivered by the positive results: quantilisers are one-shot, shielding is per-step or per-episode, tiling is a one-step licensing theorem, logical induction is asymptotic, and debate is per-claim. No found result proves an unbounded sequence of self-modifications preserves alignment.\n- The positive results are therefore best read as conditional safety theorems: they identify what would have to be true for a guarantee, and they show that the formal machinery can work in principle. They do not close the gap to a real system.\n\n### Gaps\n- No source returned simultaneously discharges the value-specification, model-fidelity, verifier-soundness, implementation, and unbounded-horizon assumptions.\n- No source returned proves alignment for a real self-improving system; the positive results are toy-model theorems or bounded-verification results.\n- No source returned establishes that the formal objective \\(U\\) used in the positive theorems is identical to, or adequately approximates, human values.\n- No source returned proves that a self-modifying agent cannot alter the specification, monitor, proof system, or utility function on which its safety guarantee depends."}}

